    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where a US lawyer would reach for a jury, prosecutorial discretion, or an automatic exclusionary rule, ask what German procedure actually puts in that exact spot instead. The three-tier Tatbestand, Rechtswidrigkeit, and Schuld structure is the analytical backbone every case runs through, and the Legalitätsprinzip determines whether a case with sufficient evidence actually reaches prosecution at all. Untersuchungshaft shows how a suspect is held, or released, pending trial without any bail system, and the Beweisverwertungsverbot's case-by-case balancing determines what evidence gathered along the way can actually be used. Rights under the StPO govern how a suspect is treated during questioning, and the Strafbefehlsverfahren resolves the great majority of minor cases without ever reaching a full trial at all. The Schöffengericht system shows what an actual trial looks like when one does happen, and Nebenklage gives certain victims a genuine, active procedural role within that same trial. Strafzumessung determines the consequence once guilt is established, and the Berufung and Revision appeal structure determines what, if anything, can still be challenged afterward. As you read any German criminal case from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to reach for a common-law tool, a jury, a bail hearing, an automatic exclusionary rule, that simply has no real counterpart in this system.",
