        whyItMatters:
          "This is a genuinely different structural approach from the US state action doctrine, which generally confines constitutional rights to government action and leaves private disputes to ordinary private law with no direct constitutional overlay at all. You should always ask, in a German private-law dispute that touches on a value like free expression or personal dignity, whether a court is actually applying an open-ended private-law standard in a way that has quietly imported a constitutional balancing analysis, since Lüth's indirect effect doctrine means the constitutional dimension can be doing real work even in a case that looks, on its face, like an ordinary contract or tort dispute between two private parties.",
        example:
          "The Lüth case itself arose from a private boycott call against a film director, and the Bundesverfassungsgericht held that the lower courts, in deciding whether the boycott call was tortious under ordinary private law, had to interpret that private-law standard in light of the boycotter's own free-expression rights, ultimately protecting the boycott call as legitimate expression rather than tortious interference. An employment dispute over a private employer's restriction on an employee's off-duty speech similarly gets analyzed by German courts through an ordinary private-law lens, an employment-contract term or a general clause like good faith, but with the employee's own constitutional free-expression interest weighed into how that private-law standard actually gets applied.",
